you focus on each plant, every petal, and the soil beneath your fingers. But when paired with meditation, it becomes even more transformative. \n\nGardening forces you to stay present, reducing stress and increasing your connection to the earth. Plus, studies show that spending time in your garden can boost your mood and mental well-being. So when you combine the act of nurturing plants with the peace of meditation, you're nourishing both your garden and your soul.\n\n## **Other Fun Ways to Celebrate Garden Meditation Day**\n\nGarden Meditation Day is all about celebrating the powerful connection between nature and mindfulness. Here are some ways to dive deeper into the experience:\n\n*   **Host a community garden meditation event:** Invite others to join you in celebrating this day! A group meditation in a local garden or park can be a beautiful experience where you share the peace of nature with others.\n*   **Share your journey online:** Have you found a beautiful meditation spot in your garden? Snap a picture and share it with fellow plant lovers! Platforms like PlantIn are great for connecting with others and sharing tips, inspiration, and mindfulness moments.\n\n![Woman Meditating with a Flower](https://strapi.myplantin.com/i_Stock_1257393878_min_cdce13526c.webp)\n\nAs you celebrate Garden Meditation Day, take a moment to truly appreciate the beauty and peace that nature offers. Meditating in your garden can calm your mind, refresh your spirit, and deepen your connection to the earth. So, find your favorite spot, take a deep breath, and let nature guide you to inner peace. Ready to give it a try? Your mind—and your garden—will thank you.\n\nAnd if you're looking for an amazing community of plant lovers, tips, and inspiration, join us on PlantIn—the ultimate space for all your gardening needs and mindfulness moments."

    @SuppressLint("SetJavaScriptEnabled")
    override fun bindView() {
        // 1. Convert Markdown sang HTML thuần
        val options = MutableDataSet()
        val parser = Parser.builder(options).build()
        val renderer = HtmlRenderer.builder(options).build()
        val htmlContent = renderer.render(parser.parse(content))
        val htmlWithFixedImages =
            "<style>img{max-width:100%; height:auto;}</style>$htmlContent"
        viewBinding.apply {
            webView.settings.apply {
                javaScriptEnabled = true
                loadsImagesAutomatically = true
                useWideViewPort = true
                loadWithOverviewMode = true
                layoutAlgorithm = WebSettings.LayoutAlgorithm.TEXT_AUTOSIZING

            }
            webView.loadDataWithBaseURL(null, htmlWithFixedImages, "text/html", "UTF-8", null)
        }
    }

}